===Etymology===
{{uncertain|en|Origin uncertain}}.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=9606720343|date=July 2023|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|entry=shenanigans|pos=n}}</ref> As the earliest attestations date from California, U.S.A, in the 1850s (see the quotations) towards the end of the {{w|California gold rush}}, it is possible that the word derives from one of the following:<ref name="WWW">{{R:World Wide Words|url=https://www.worldwidewords.org/ww-she1.html|date_created=18 December 1999|date_updated=1 April 2012}}</ref>

* {{der|en|ga|sionnachuighim|t=to play tricks|lit=to play the fox}}; Irishmen were among the people participating in the gold rush. (See also the 31 December 1854 quotation suggesting it is an “Irish word”.)
* {{der|en|es|chanada}}, a shortening of {{m|es|charranada|t=deceit, trick}}; California was colonized by the {{w|Spanish Empire}} in the 18th century, and many people from Latin America also took part in the gold rush.

Other suggestions are set out in the table below.

{{col-top|1|possible etymologies}}
* From the [[w:East Anglian English|East Anglian]] dialectal word {{m|en|nannicking|t=playing the fool}}.<ref name="WWW"/>
* From {{noncog|fr|manigance|ces manigances|t=these fraudulent schemes}}.
* From {{noncog|de|Scheinheilige|t=sham holy men; sham holy actions}}, {{m|de|scheinheilig|t=hypocritical}} {{qualifier|18th c.}}
* From {{noncog|gmw-rfr|schinägeln|t=to work hard}}, from the peddler’s argot term {{m|gmw-rfr||Schenigelei|t=work}}.<ref name="WWW"/>
{{col-bottom}}

